Regression test for the checkboard unregister-vs-reply race.

A replicant registers an entry in the checkboard for each sorese transaction
and waits on it for the commit rc.  When the rc arrives, the master's reply is
handled by the net reader thread, not the sql thread:

  osql_chkboard_sqlsession_rc()          osql_unregister_sqlthr()
    lock   checkboard->mtx
    fetch  entry
    lock   entry->mtx
    unlock checkboard->mtx
                                           delete_from_checkerboard()
                                             lock/unlock checkboard->mtx
                                           cleanup_entry()
                                             destroy entry->mtx   <-- held!
    ...
    unlock entry->mtx

The handoff from checkboard->mtx to entry->mtx keeps the lookup safe, but it
does not stop the sql thread from unregistering right afterwards.  If it does,
cleanup_entry() destroys and frees an entry the reader thread is still inside.
pthread_mutex_destroy() on a held mutex returns EBUSY, so Pthread_mutex_destroy()
aborts -- a core on the replicant, in cleanup_entry().

The window is the few hundred nanoseconds between the reader's unlock of
checkboard->mtx and its unlock of entry->mtx, and the sql thread has to arrive
inside it.  Nothing the test can do from the outside aligns two threads that
finely: the coarsest knob available, sleep_before_dispatch, has one-second
granularity, and the replicant's commit-rc timer starts before the bplog is
even shipped, so a reply timed against it is always tens of milliseconds late
and the reader simply fails the lookup.  That is the benign outcome, and it is
the only one a purely timing-driven test ever gets.

So the test widens each half instead, with two internal tunables that exist for
this purpose, and lets the wide versions overlap deterministically:

  sleep_before_dispatch (master)          stalls the block processor, so the
                                          reply is sent STALL seconds late.

  sleep_after_commitrc_timeout            parks the sql thread after it gives
    (replicant)                           up, before it unregisters, so the
                                          entry is still in the hash when the
                                          reply lands.

  sleep_in_sqlsession_rc (replicant)      holds entry->mtx in the reader across
                                          the point where the sql thread
                                          unregisters.

  SOSQL_MAX_COMMIT_WAIT_SEC (lrl)         when the sql thread gives up.

  survive_n_master_swings (lrl)           0, so the commit-wait timeout is
                                          terminal and we abandon exactly one
                                          transaction instead of retrying.

Timeline, with the values in runit (t=0 is the COMMIT):

  t=0.0   replicant ships the bplog and starts waiting (TIMEOUT=2)
  t=2.0   sql thread times out, logs, then parks for HOLD_UNREG=5
  t=2.0+  master finishes its STALL=2 and sends the rc
  t=2.0+  reader finds the entry, takes entry->mtx, parks for HOLD_RC=8
  t=7.0   sql thread wakes, unregisters, and reaches cleanup_entry
            without the fix: destroys a mutex the reader holds -> EBUSY -> core
            with the fix:    blocks on entry->mtx until the reader is done
  t=10.0+ reader releases, cleanup_entry proceeds, COMMIT returns

Both overlaps have seconds of slack, so the ordering does not depend on luck.

The COMMIT is expected to fail -- the transaction was abandoned -- but it must
not return before the reader has let go of the entry.  Its wall time is the
assertion that the race was actually set up: ~10s means the reply did find the
entry and cleanup_entry waited for it, which is exactly the path under test.
A run that returns in ~7s would mean the reply never landed in the window and
the test proved nothing, so that is a failure too.

Note that osql_checkboard_update_status() (the poke path) hands off between the
same two mutexes, and races unregister the same way.  It is not covered here.
